{"ts":"2026-08-16T13:40:33.120Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"implementer","message":"docs: SC1087 fix verified clean — shellcheck errors 0, warnings 0, bash -n clean, escape output byte-identical (^[[1m bold ^[[0m^[[38;5;150m green ^[[0m), cockpit.sh renders and exits 0. Good.\n\nBut running it end-to-end surfaced a SEPARATE real bug in the same file. cockpit.sh:37-43 — the python3 snippet uses json and sys but never imports them:\n\n  eval \"$(herdr agent list 2>/dev/null | python3 -c \"\n  try: a=json.load(sys.stdin)['result'].get('agents',[])\n  except Exception: a=[]\n  ...\n\nNameError on json/sys is caught by the bare `except Exception`, so it silently falls through to a=[] and the cockpit ALWAYS prints \"0 agent(s)\".\n\nProof:\n  $ herdr agent list | python3 -c \"<snippet as written>\"   → AGENTS=0\n  $ herdr agent list | python3 -c \"import json,sys; <same>\" → AGENTS=4;WORKING=2;BLOCKED=0\n  $ herdr agent list | grep -o '\"agent_status\":\"[a-z]*\"' | sort | uniq -c → 2 idle, 2 working\n\nLive cockpit header right now reads \"cockpit ● running · 0 agent(s)\" while 4 agents are attached and 2 are working.\n\nFix: add `import json,sys` at the top of the -c snippet. Worth also narrowing that bare `except Exception` — it's what hid this. This matters for public release: cockpit.sh is the front door we tell new users to run, and it currently under-reports the team as empty. Yours (script correctness). I've logged it in build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T13:41:15.203Z","from":"implementer","action":"send","to":"docs","message":"Fixed. cockpit.sh:37-43 — added `import json,sys`, narrowed bare `except Exception` to `(ValueError, KeyError)` so a real bug fails loud instead of silently reading as \"no agents\". Commit ebc224d. Verified: your exact repro now returns AGENTS=4;WORKING=2;BLOCKED=0 (was 0 before). Ran full ./cockpit.sh live — header now reads \"4 agent(s), 2 working\", matches `herdr agent list` ground truth. bash -n clean, shellcheck 0 errors/0 warnings. Good catch — that would've shipped a broken front door."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T13:41:15.204Z","from":"implementer","action":"send","to":"verifier","message":"Fixed the cockpit \"always 0 agents\" bug docs found (build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md). cockpit.sh:37-43 missing `import json,sys`, bare except swallowed the NameError. Commit ebc224d. Verified live: ./cockpit.sh header now reads \"4 agent(s), 2 working\" (was \"0 agent(s)\"). Please independent-confirm for the pre-release check / EVIDENCE.md."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T13:41:18.372Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"human","message":"docs: implementer's shellcheck fix verified independently — 0 errors, 0 warnings, bash -n clean, escape output byte-identical, cockpit.sh runs and exits 0. Criterion 2 now met.\n\nBut running cockpit.sh end-to-end (rather than just linting it) found a SECOND, worse bug in the same file. cockpit.sh:37-43 calls python3 with a snippet that uses json and sys without importing them. The NameError is swallowed by a bare `except Exception`, so the agent count falls through to 0 — silently, on every run, always.\n\nLive header right now: \"cockpit ● running · 0 agent(s)\" while 4 agents are attached and 2 are working. Ground truth from herdr agent list: 4 agents, 2 working. It has never worked.\n\nThis one matters more than the shellcheck issue: README tells new users cockpit.sh is the command that \"knows what to do next\", and it currently tells them their team is empty while it's running. Sent to implementer with the fix (add `import json,sys`, and narrow that bare except — the broad catch is what hid it).\n\nUpdated build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md: 7 of 9 checks pass now. Remaining: this cockpit count bug (#9), and the lead-agent drop (#8) still needs your call — fix or document.\n\nWorth noting: linting alone would never have caught #9. It only showed up because I ran the thing."}
