{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:10:36.273Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"implementer","message":"docs: c425783 verified — good fix, and you were right to abandon title-matching. I confirmed the truncation independently: w1:p7/p8/pA all sit at len=67 ending \"...harness/build\" with the script name already cut off in THIS session, so title matching was broken before it shipped. Keying on tab_id is the right call — side tabs (t2/t3/t4/t6) can never collide with the main tab (t1) where agents live.\n\nRan your root_pane() live: correctly exits 1 instead of claiming w1:p2. shellcheck 0/0, bash -n clean.\n\nOne consequence you should know about, already handled on my side: your fix CHANGED the documented recovery. Before, --resume would (destructively) always find a pane; now it can refuse with \"could not find a shell pane to start the lead in\" (build.sh:256). My troubleshooting entry said \"--resume re-attaches a lead\", which is now incomplete. Updated in 06e23bf — the refusal is documented as the safe outcome, with stop-then-resume as the follow-up, and the old hijack behaviour kept as a historical note for anyone on a pre-c425783 checkout.\n\nNothing further needed from you. Thread 2 (why the lead pane vanished) stays open and documented in build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md section 8 — needs a throwaway session, human's call."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:10:48.149Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"human","message":"docs: (a) thread 1 done and verified. The pane-hijack bug is fixed (c425783) — ./build.sh --resume can no longer eat your team-chat pane. I verified it live: it now correctly refuses instead of claiming w1:p2.\n\nWorth flagging how that fix landed, because it's a good sign about the process: implementer's first attempt matched terminal titles, then live testing showed herdr truncates them — 3 of 4 side-tab panes in this very session already had their script name cut off. Title matching would have shipped broken and failed silently later, past some path length. They caught it by running it, not reading it, and switched to tab_id. That's the same reason the cockpit \"0 agents\" bug only surfaced when I ran the script rather than linted it.\n\nTheir fix also changed my documented recovery — --resume can now refuse rather than always finding a pane — so I updated troubleshooting.md to match (06e23bf), keeping the old behaviour as a historical note for anyone on an older checkout.\n\nState now: 5 commits ahead of origin, working tree clean, shellcheck 0/0, bash -n clean across 18 scripts.\n\nThread 2 — WHY the lead vanished — is the one thing still open. It needs a throwaway session and some spend to reproduce, and I've written down everything known about it in build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md section 8 so it's not silently dropped: pane gone entirely (not just its label), stale lead.pane pointer, and intercom failing for lead from the very first message, which hints it never registered rather than died. build.sh's own comments admit the /name registration timing is fragile.\n\nMy read: you can ship without closing thread 2 — it's documented, it has a recovery, and the destructive part is fixed. But it IS a real coordination failure and it happened on the first run we watched closely, so it'll happen to a user eventually. Your call: chase it now, or file it as a follow-up and push these 5 commits."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:14:14.783Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"human","message":"docs: pushed. origin/main 14be948 → 06e23bf, 5 commits:\n\n  7386410  cockpit.sh: brace ANSI palette vars (shellcheck SC1087)\n  ebc224d  cockpit.sh: fix always reporting 0 agents\n  374c111  docs: add lead-loss recovery to troubleshooting\n  c425783  build.sh: root_pane() no longer claims occupied panes\n  06e23bf  docs: update lead-loss recovery for the root_pane() fix\n\nVerified after push: local matches origin/main, tree clean, nothing ahead.\n\nPublic-facing state: 8 of 9 pre-release checks pass, 9th (why the lead pane vanished) is documented as open in build/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md section 8 rather than dropped. Evidence for every check is in that file with the actual command output.\n\nTwo things I'd suggest as follow-ups, neither blocking:\n1. Thread 2 — the lead-vanishing root cause. Needs a throwaway session.\n2. docs/monitoring-agents.md:52 still tells readers to run `herdr agent explain <agent-name-or-pane-id>`. Neither form works on this install — I documented the working alternative in troubleshooting.md but left that file alone since it wasn't my assignment. Worth a one-line correction by whoever owns it.\n\nDone unless you want either picked up."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:21:27.601Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"implementer","message":"docs: fixed the herdr agent targeting docs (c14ea50, pushed). Turned out bigger than one line — four files, not one.\n\nFindings, all verified live: role names return agent_not_found for get/read/wait/explain (tested against three live named agents: docs, verifier, researcher). Only pane_id resolves. `herdr agent explain` is worse — BOTH target forms fail; the working form is `--file <buffer> --agent <backend>`, where --agent names the manifest (claude/codex), not the role. Also found herdr/setup.md had `herdr agent start <agent> <pane>` when the real signature per --help is `<NAME> --kind <KIND> --pane <ID>`.\n\nOne item in YOUR lane, not urgent and not blocking: scripts/launch-feature.sh:81-83 prints a \"LIVE mode would run\" block using the name form —\n  herdr agent prompt <name> \"...\"\n  herdr agent wait  <name> --until done --until blocked --timeout 900000\nThe `agent start <name> --kind claude --pane <pane>` line above it is correct, but the prompt/wait lines would fail with agent_not_found if anyone followed them. It's descriptive heredoc text rather than executed code, so nothing breaks today — but it's advice that doesn't work. Your call whether to switch it to pane ids.\n\nWorth noting the same line's comment at launch-feature.sh:16-17 already half-knew this (\"herdr agent wait resolves only recognized live agents\")."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:21:37.231Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"researcher","message":"docs: heads up on a gap in the ground-truth audits, no action needed from you unless you want it — I've already fixed the docs.\n\nresearch/mission-groundtruth-2026-08-15.md:27 marks this VERIFIED:\n  | 7 | docs/monitoring-agents.md:74 | `herdr agent explain <agent>` diagnoses state | `herdr agent explain --help` | VERIFIED |\n\nThe evidence column is `herdr agent explain --help`. That proves the subcommand exists and its help text renders — it does not prove the documented invocation works. I ran the actual command: `herdr agent explain <role-name>` returns agent_not_found, and `herdr agent explain <pane-id>` returns agent_explain_unavailable. Both documented forms fail on Herdr 0.8.0. The only working form is `--file <buffer> --agent <backend>`.\n\nSame pattern for the role-name targeting generally: get/read/wait all reject role names with agent_not_found, verified against three live named agents. Fixed across 4 files in c14ea50.\n\nNot a criticism of the audit method — --help checking is the right cheap first pass and it caught real drift elsewhere. Just flagging the class: for claims of the form \"command X does Y\", --help proves X exists, not that Y happens. Might be worth a column in future audits distinguishing \"exists\" from \"invocation verified\", since the 08-16 re-audit inherited the VERIFIED status from the 08-15 file for this row.\n\nbuild/PRE-RELEASE-CHECK.md has the full evidence if you want it."}
{"ts":"2026-08-16T14:21:46.073Z","from":"docs","action":"send","to":"human","message":"docs: #2 corrected and pushed — c14ea50, origin/main now at c14ea50.\n\nIt was bigger than the one line I flagged. Four files told readers to address agents by role name, and that fails on Herdr 0.8.0 — get, read, wait and explain all return agent_not_found. Verified against three live named agents. Only the pane_id from `herdr agent list` works.\n\n`herdr agent explain` was doubly wrong: BOTH documented forms fail. The form that actually works is `--file <captured-buffer> --agent <backend>`, where --agent names the manifest (claude/codex), not the role — passing a role name returns unknown_agent. Documented the capture-then-explain recipe, plus a caveat that explain replays the screen-manifest fallback so it can legitimately disagree with `herdr agent list` when lifecycle hooks are installed; hooks win.\n\nAlso caught while I was in there: herdr/setup.md had `herdr agent start <agent> <pane>`; the real signature per --help is `<NAME> --kind <KIND> --pane <ID>`.\n\nEvery command in the changed sections was executed against the live session before I wrote it down.\n\nTwo follow-ups sent, neither blocking:\n- implementer: scripts/launch-feature.sh:81-83 prints \"LIVE mode would run\" advice using the name form. Descriptive text, nothing breaks today, but the advice doesn't work.\n- researcher: the 08-15 audit marked this claim VERIFIED on the evidence of `herdr agent explain --help`. That proves the subcommand exists, not that the documented invocation works — and the 08-16 re-audit inherited the status. Worth splitting \"exists\" from \"invocation verified\" in future audits.\n\nNothing else open from me."}
